You can use a loop to run the same code on multiple variables. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 20, c = 30;

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    int vars[] = {a, b, c};
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        std::cout << "Variable " << i << ": " << vars[i] << std::endl;
        // Run the same code on each variable
        vars[i] *= 2;
        std::cout << "Variable " << i << " after operation: " << vars[i] << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

However, if you want to run the same code on multiple named variables, you can use a map or an unordered_map to store the variables and their names. Here's an example:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> variables = {
        {"a", 10},
        {"b", 20},
        {"c", 30}
    };

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    for (auto& pair : variables) {
        std::cout << "Variable " << pair.first << ": " << pair.second << std::endl;
        // Run the same code on each variable
        pair.second *= 2;
        std::cout << "Variable " << pair.first << " after operation: " << pair.second << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This way, you can easily add or remove variables from the map and run the same code on them.

Alternatively, you can use a struct to store the variable name and value, and then use a loop to run the same code on each variable:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

struct Variable {
    std::string name;
    int value;
};

int main() {
    Variable variables[] = {
        {"a", 10},
        {"b", 20},
        {"c", 30}
    };

    // Using a loop to run the same code on multiple variables
    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
        std::cout << "Variable " << variables[i].name << ": " << variables[i].value << std::endl;
        // Run the same code on each variable
        variables[i].value *= 2;
        std::cout << "Variable " << variables[i].name << " after operation: " << variables[i].value << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

This approach is more flexible and can be used with any type of variable, not just integers.

